WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
In July 1996, a white-faced lamb was born

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.000
in Scotland to a black-faced ewe.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.720
She was Dolly, and she wasn't that ewe's

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:09.200
daughter.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.960
She was a clone of a different sheep.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.160
Meanwhile, labs were routinely cloning genes, copying one

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.440
piece of DNA billions of times inside bacteria.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.960
So what does each kind of cloning copy,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.120
how is the copy made, and how well

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.200
does each do the job it's meant for?

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.520
A clone is a genetically identical copy.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.000
The question is, a copy of what?

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.520
You've met natural clones already: a banana plant

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.440
grown from a piece of its parent has

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.520
the same alleles as that parent.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.360
Whole-organism cloning aims to make a new individual

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.240
with the same DNA as an existing one.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.200
Gene cloning makes many copies of just one

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.720
selected piece of DNA.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.200
So always ask what is being copied: a

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.560
whole individual, or one gene.

00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:02.086
Here's how Dolly was made, by a method

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:04.726
called somatic cell nuclear transfer.

00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:08.086
Somatic just means a body cell, not an

00:01:08.247 --> 00:01:09.646
egg or a sperm.

00:01:10.087 --> 00:01:12.406
Scientists took cells grown from the udder of

00:01:12.487 --> 00:01:14.246
a white-faced ewe.

00:01:14.327 --> 00:01:16.486
Then they took an egg from a black-faced

00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:19.126
ewe, and sucked out its nucleus, so the

00:01:19.207 --> 00:01:21.846
egg had no chromosomes of its own.

00:01:21.887 --> 00:01:24.246
An electric pulse fused the udder cell with

00:01:24.327 --> 00:01:27.206
the empty egg and started it dividing.

00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:29.606
After about a week, the young embryo was

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:32.326
placed in the uterus of another black-faced ewe,

00:01:32.407 --> 00:01:33.686
who carried it to birth.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.860
So Dolly had three mothers.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:38.500
Which one does she match?

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.620
Her nucleus came from the white-faced ewe, so

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.740
her chromosomes, and her white face, match the

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.540
nucleus donor, not the egg donor or the

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.220
ewe who gave birth.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.380
But she wasn't a perfect copy.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.020
Her mitochondria, which carry a little DNA of

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.340
their own, came from the egg.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.660
And a clone develops in a different uterus

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.980
and grows up in its own environment, so

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.860
it can differ in size, markings or health.

00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:08.026
Now assess it, starting with reliability.

00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:10.986
The team at the Roslin Institute fused 277

00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:13.946
udder cells with eggs.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:17.146
Only 29 grew into embryos healthy enough to

00:02:17.227 --> 00:02:20.346
transfer, and just one became a live lamb.

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:21.786
Why so few?

00:02:22.347 --> 00:02:25.386
As an udder cell specialised, chemical tags, like

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:27.866
the ones on histones, switched many of its

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.106
genes off.

00:02:29.387 --> 00:02:31.546
To start a whole new animal, the egg

00:02:31.627 --> 00:02:34.186
has to reset those switches, and it often

00:02:34.307 --> 00:02:35.626
does that only partly.

00:02:36.187 --> 00:02:38.986
So many clones die before or soon after

00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:42.106
birth, and some are born unusually large.

00:02:42.587 --> 00:02:45.866
Rates have improved, but most attempts still fail.

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:48.506
What about Dolly's health?

00:02:48.987 --> 00:02:51.866
She developed arthritis, and at six she was

00:02:51.947 --> 00:02:53.946
put down because of a lung disease caused

00:02:54.027 --> 00:02:56.666
by a virus that also infected other sheep

00:02:56.707 --> 00:02:57.706
in her flock.

00:02:58.027 --> 00:03:01.106
That alone can't show cloning aged her early.

00:03:01.387 --> 00:03:04.106
And four later clones from the same udder

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:06.106
cells were healthy for their age at seven

00:03:06.187 --> 00:03:06.746
to nine.

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:10.106
So effectiveness depends on the purpose.

00:03:10.347 --> 00:03:13.546
Cloning is too unreliable for everyday breeding, but

00:03:13.627 --> 00:03:15.386
it can be worth it to copy one

00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:16.506
rare animal.

00:03:16.827 --> 00:03:20.346
Cells from a black-footed ferret, frozen in 1988,

00:03:20.427 --> 00:03:23.546
have given clones, and in 2024 one of

00:03:23.627 --> 00:03:27.066
them had babies, passing on alleles previously absent

00:03:27.147 --> 00:03:28.586
from the breeding population.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.840
Gene cloning has a narrower aim: lots of

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:34.720
copies of one gene.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.720
Scientists put the gene into a plasmid, a

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.840
small ring of DNA that bacteria carry.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.880
Here the plasmid is a vector, a carrier

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.520
that moves DNA into a cell.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.520
The bacterium that takes it up is the

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:49.800
host.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.280
Each time the host divides, it copies the

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.840
plasmid as well as its own DNA, so

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.960
the new cells get the gene.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:00.120
Bacteria can divide every half hour or so,

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.680
so by the next day one cell becomes

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.040
billions.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:06.200
There's also a way to copy a gene

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.920
with no cells, called PCR.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.000
Heat separates the DNA's two strands, then, as

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.520
it cools, an enzyme copies each one, so

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.680
every cycle doubles the gene.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.779
So how effective is it?

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.660
Very, for its purpose.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.780
Only a few bacteria take up a plasmid,

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.740
but you need just one, because it multiplies.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.100
The result is easy to check, because scientists

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.180
can read the copied gene's sequence.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.380
The copies let scientists study a gene, make

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.900
its protein, or put it into another organism.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.940
Its limits are small: the gene doesn't always

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.260
end up in the plasmid, so colonies must

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:47.980
be checked.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.600
To tell them apart, ask what exists at

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:53.680
the end that didn't before.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.040
A new organism means whole-organism cloning.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.560
Many copies of one gene means gene cloning.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.320
Two things to hold on to.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.040
The clone matches the nucleus donor.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.360
And gene cloning copies a gene; organism cloning

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:10.880
copies an individual.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.540
Two exam questions now.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:14.700
First.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:18.660
A company made 100 embryos by nuclear transfer,

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.300
using skin cells from a prize dairy bull.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:24.500
Six calves were born, and four were alive

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:25.300
at one year.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.700
Their black and white patches differed from the

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:29.460
bull's, and from each other's.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:32.900
Assess the effectiveness of cloning for copying this

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:33.300
bull.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.220
Pause here to answer.

00:05:45.634 --> 00:05:49.153
Reliability is low: four live calves from a

00:05:49.234 --> 00:05:51.713
hundred embryos, and two of the six born

00:05:51.794 --> 00:05:52.833
died.

00:05:52.954 --> 00:05:56.033
Their nuclear DNA matches the bull's, but patches

00:05:56.114 --> 00:05:59.553
differ, because patch patterns form during development, not

00:05:59.634 --> 00:06:01.073
from genes alone.

00:06:01.114 --> 00:06:02.913
So it's worth it only for a very

00:06:03.074 --> 00:06:04.513
valuable bull.

00:06:04.554 --> 00:06:07.713
Saying different patches prove the DNA wasn't copied

00:06:07.794 --> 00:06:09.233
is the classic mistake.

00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:10.913
Second.

00:06:10.994 --> 00:06:13.873
A zoo stores frozen skin cells from a

00:06:13.954 --> 00:06:16.273
northern white rhino that has died.

00:06:16.594 --> 00:06:18.993
Project A will make many copies of one

00:06:19.074 --> 00:06:21.633
of the rhino's genes, to study its sequence.

00:06:22.274 --> 00:06:25.233
Project B aims to produce a living rhino

00:06:25.314 --> 00:06:26.753
calf from the cells.

00:06:27.394 --> 00:06:29.633
Distinguish between the cloning used in the two

00:06:29.794 --> 00:06:30.433
projects.

00:06:30.994 --> 00:06:32.273
Pause here to answer.

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:44.940
Project A is gene cloning.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.740
The gene goes into a plasmid, and dividing

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.700
bacteria copy it, so the product is many

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:52.540
copies of one gene.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:55.860
Project B is whole-organism cloning.

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:58.460
A skin cell's nucleus goes into an egg

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.340
with its own nucleus removed, so the product

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:04.100
is a calf whose nuclear DNA matches the

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:05.100
rhino's.

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:07.420
Saying both projects make a copy of the

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.580
rhino is the classic mistake.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.680
So when a question says cloning, first ask

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:16.960
what is being copied: a whole individual, or

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.040
one gene.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:20.800
Then trace how the copy is made, and

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.520
judge it against its purpose: how reliable it

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.240
is, how identical the copies are, and how

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.400
healthy or useful they are.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.520
Next, how scientists cut a gene out, join

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:33.760
it into a plasmid, and get it working

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:34.880
inside a host.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:37.540
Here are the key notes.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:39.700
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:40.580
to copy them.
